from pathlib import Path
p=Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
captions={
'01_pipeline.svg':'**Figure 1 — Planning pipeline.** The Scheduler expands jobs, resolves tray moves, assigns AGVs, reduces and validates the event DAG, then creates timed motion and output actions. The figure describes phase ordering, not a ROS executor schedule.',
'02_target_architecture.svg':'**Figure 2 — Proposed architecture.** Solver contracts separate policy from orchestration; concrete default solvers may still depend on graph, collision, and cost services. This is a proposed design, not an implementation already present.',
'03_garage_xy.svg':'**Figure 3 — Top-down garage setup.** AGV 1 faces the positive $x$ direction. Trays T2 and T3 lie along the illustrative route to the goal. Coordinates are schematic; node IDs and distances are not an actual customer layout.',
'04_dependencies.svg':'**Figure 4 — Event dependencies.** An arrow means the source event must complete before the destination event can execute. Alternative topological orders may still satisfy the same DAG.',
'05_agv_reposition.svg':'**Figure 5 — AGV approach and carry.** The blue route repositions AGV 1 to tray T7; after lift, the green route moves the tray to the destination. The Scheduler represents those actions at different abstraction levels.',
'06_blocking_resolution.svg':'**Figure 6 — Clearing a blocking tray.** The amber relocation of the blocking tray is a prerequisite to the green requested tray move. The figure illustrates why adding an event without adding the dependency is unsafe.',
'07_agv_collision.svg':'**Figure 7 — Competing arrivals.** Two AGVs approach an overlapping corridor or node. The assignment planner must impose a departure-before-arrival dependency when an occupancy conflict would otherwise occur. The red region is illustrative, not a measured collision shape.',
'08_uml_solver.svg':'**Figure 8 — Proposed UML class structure.** `Scheduler` uses the `TrayMoveSolver` interface, which `DefaultTrayMoveSolver` implements. The diagram reference-based ownership is one possible first-pass design.',
'09_sequence_tray.svg':'**Figure 9 — Proposed tray solver interaction.** The Scheduler delegates to the solver, which reads the input event ordering and adds moves and dependencies to the event DAG before returning a diagnostic result.'}
for fn in ['01-software-background.md','02-issue-and-proposal.md','03-verification-plan.md']:
 f=p/fn; s=f.read_text()
 import re
 s=re.sub(r'(!\[[^\]]*\]\(figures/([^\)]+\.svg)\))',lambda m: m.group(1)+'\n\n'+captions.get(m.group(2),''),s)
 f.write_text(s)

f=p/'01-software-background.md'
s=f.read_text()
insert='''\n## 10. Walkthrough: concrete C++ code from the snapshot\n\n### 10.1 Construct a scheduling context\n\nThe following extracts the same APIs used by the existing unit tests. The test constructs an initial discrete state, instantiates `Scheduler`, creates a `TrayMove`, and adds it to the DAG. The example is abbreviated; `state` and `layout_filepath` must already be initialized from valid fixtures.\n\n```cpp\nScheduler scheduler(layout_filepath,\n                    rclcpp::get_logger("scheduler"),\n                    SchedulerParams{});\n\nauto ctx = std::make_shared<ScheduleContext>(\n    volley::MakeGuid(0, 0xDEADBEEF), state, rclcpp::Time());\n\nauto move = std::make_shared<volley::TrayMove>(0u);\nmove->SetTrayId(1);\nmove->SetDest(volley::ConstructGaragePose(1, 90));\nctx->events.AddEvent(move);\n\nauto [success, message] = scheduler.DoTrayAssignments(ctx);\n```
\nThe important C++ concepts are **type inference** (`auto`), **shared ownership** (`std::make_shared`), **structured bindings** (`auto [success, message]`), and **polymorphism** (`std::shared_ptr<TrayMove>` converted to an event pointer for the DAG). This snippet reflects API usage, not a complete stand-alone program. Reference: `src/planner/scheduler/test/scheduler_tests.cpp`, `TrayAssignmentTestComplexDag` and `UnblockingTrayMoveTest`.\n\n### 10.2 DAG events and runtime dispatch\n\nThe observed core interface includes the following declarations:\n\n```cpp\nclass ScheduleEvent {\n public:\n  virtual ~ScheduleEvent() = default;\n  virtual BoolStringPair Apply(DiscreteGarageState& state) const = 0;\n  virtual std::optional<GarageEntityIdMsg> GetGarageEntityId() const = 0;\n};\n\n// In the scheduler's traversal of event vertices:\nauto event = ctx->events.GetEventFromVertex(event_vert).value();\nif (event->GetType() == ScheduleEventType::TRAY_MOVE) {\n  auto* move = dynamic_cast<volley::TrayMove*>(event.get());\n  // Process the tray-specific event ...\n}\n```
\nThis combines a shortened base-class declaration with a representative branch from `DoTrayAssignments`; it is **not** a literal continuous source excerpt. `virtual` enables runtime dispatch, the pure-virtual marker `= 0` makes the base abstract, `dynamic_cast` checks a downcast at runtime, and `std::optional` allows the entity identifier to be absent. The existing code tests the event type before its downcast.\n\n### 10.3 Why shared pointers make a naïve snapshot unsafe\n\n```cpp\nauto dag_copy = ctx->events;        // Copy DAG structure\nauto original_move = move;          // Shared event object\noriginal_move->SetTrayId(42);       // Visible through aliased pointers\n```
\nThis is illustrative C++. Since `DagBase<T>` stores `std::shared_ptr<T>`, copying the DAG structure does **not** deep-copy each event. A benchmark comparing two solvers must construct independent fixtures or perform a deliberate deep clone of event objects and edges.\n\n## 11. Annotated AGV scenes\n\n![Approach and carry](figures/05_agv_reposition.svg)\n\n'''+captions['05_agv_reposition.svg']+'''\n\nIn the approach stage, the AGV position $\\mathbf p_a\\in\\mathbb R^2$ and tray position $\\mathbf p_t\\in\\mathbb R^2$ determine the desired travel. Here $\\mathbf p_a=[x_a,y_a]^{\\mathsf T}$ and $\\mathbf p_t=[x_t,y_t]^{\\mathsf T}$, where $x_a,y_a,x_t,y_t\\in\\mathbb R$ are planar coordinates in meters. The geometric displacement $\\Delta\\mathbf p=\\mathbf p_t-\\mathbf p_a\\in\\mathbb R^2$ is illustrative; the actual planner computes paths through directed, heading-aware graph vertices rather than simply following a straight line.\n\n![Blocking tray](figures/06_blocking_resolution.svg)\n\n'''+captions['06_blocking_resolution.svg']+'''\n\nThe obstacle's move must precede the requested move: for event vertices $u,v\\in\\mathcal V$, the dependency $(u,v)\\in\\mathcal E$ requires $\\pi(u)<\\pi(v)$, where the ordering map $\\pi:\\mathcal V\\to\\{0,\\ldots,n-1\\}$ and event count $n\\in\\mathbb N$ are defined in Section 3. This dependency is an ordering constraint, not a statement that two actions must happen with zero delay.\n\n![Competing AGVs](figures/07_agv_collision.svg)\n\n'''+captions['07_agv_collision.svg']+'''\n\nThe inter-AGV separation $d=\\lVert\\mathbf p_A-\\mathbf p_B\\rVert_2\\in\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}$ can be visualized in 2D, with AGV locations $\\mathbf p_A,\\mathbf p_B\\in\\mathbb R^2$. In production, feasibility is checked using occupancy, collision geometry, and event ordering—not a distance-only rule.\n'''
s+='\n'+insert
f.write_text(s)

f=p/'02-issue-and-proposal.md';s=f.read_text();s+='''\n## 10. Proposed UML and method sequence\n\n![Solver UML](figures/08_uml_solver.svg)\n\n'''+captions['08_uml_solver.svg']+'''\n\nThe design uses the **Strategy** pattern: `Scheduler` calls a stable solver interface without embedding the concrete algorithm. The relationship from `DefaultTrayMoveSolver` to `TrayMoveSolver` is interface realization (dashed line with hollow triangle). A test fake can implement the same interface.\n\n![Solver call sequence](figures/09_sequence_tray.svg)\n\n'''+captions['09_sequence_tray.svg']+'''\n\n### C++ contract and calling code (proposed)\n\n```cpp\nstruct TraySolveRequest {\n  const DiscreteGarageState& initial_state;\n  ScheduleEventDag& events;\n  std::unordered_map<uint16_t, ScheduleEventSPtr>& first_tray_event;\n};\n\nclass TrayMoveSolver {\n public:\n  virtual ~TrayMoveSolver() = default;\n  virtual BoolStringPair Solve(TraySolveRequest request) const = 0;\n};\n\n// Illustrative Scheduler integration after dependency injection:\nTraySolveRequest request{ctx->initial_state, ctx->events,\n                         ctx->first_tray_event};\nauto [success, diagnostic] = tray_solver_->Solve(request);\n```
\nThese names and signatures are *proposals*, not existing repository declarations. The `const` reference to `initial_state` prevents changing the initial state through this argument, whereas the DAG and first-tray-event map are intentionally mutable. Alternative implementations can reuse the same contract, but an algorithm that needs different environment services will require constructor injection or a revised request representation.\n\n### Representative current algorithm fragment (from `DoTrayAssignments`)\n\n```cpp\nauto working_state = ctx->initial_state;\nauto total_ordering_opt = ctx->events.GetTopologicalOrdering();\nif(!total_ordering_opt.has_value()) {\n  return std::make_pair(false, "Failed to get total ordering");\n}\n\nvolley::GarageOccupancyChecker<\n    std::shared_ptr<volley::ScheduleEvent>> freeing_moves(table_, gg_);\n```
\nThis fragment demonstrates why a default implementation needs graph and collision dependencies and why the algorithm's mutable working state should not be confused with the immutable initial state.\n''';f.write_text(s)

f=p/'03-verification-plan.md';s=f.read_text();s+='''\n## 9. Example behavior-regression assertions\n\nThe current repository has tests verifying `TrayMove` resolution and `DoAgvAssignments`. A parity harness should compare semantic content, not only success flags. The following is schematic GoogleTest code, not a drop-in existing test:\n\n```cpp\nEXPECT_EQ(reference.success, candidate.success);\nEXPECT_EQ(reference.moves.size(), candidate.moves.size());\nfor (size_t i = 0; i < reference.moves.size(); ++i) {\n  EXPECT_EQ(reference.moves[i].tray_id, candidate.moves[i].tray_id);\n  EXPECT_EQ(reference.moves[i].destination, candidate.moves[i].destination);\n}\nEXPECT_EQ(reference.dependencies, candidate.dependencies);\nEXPECT_EQ(reference.first_tray_event, candidate.first_tray_event);\n```
\n`reference` and `candidate` above are hypothetical normalized result structures. In actual tests, the last map must be canonicalized to stable event identifiers rather than compared as pointer values. Generated event order and semantic prerequisite reachability should be recorded independently; a topological sort alone does not establish a unique execution order.\n\nFor a runtime sample $t_k\\in\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}$ (seconds), with repetition index $k\\in\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ and repetition count $N\\in\\mathbb N_{>0}$, report the median $\\widetilde{t}\\in\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}$ and sample dispersion rather than a single best time. Preserve layout provenance and build revision with each result.\n''';f.write_text(s)

f=p/'README.md';s=f.read_text();s+='''\n\n## Updated diagram style\n\nAll newly generated diagrams reuse the AGV top-down icon and dark technical palette from the attached `figures.zip` reference. Source drawing utilities are in `scripts/draw.py` and `scripts/figstyle.py`; rerun `python scripts/generate_figures.py` from any working directory. The script requires Matplotlib and NumPy. Font preference is `JetBrains Mono` with `DejaVu Sans Mono` fallback (no font file is bundled). The figures use inline Markdown captions below each image. SVG output is vector-based and editable.\n''';f.write_text(s)
